# Attack: classic UAF on weak reference. Does Weak.upgrade() return None after
# the last Shared is dropped, or does it read freed memory? Also exercises
# Option[Shared[T]] drop codegen (audit originally found a void**/GorgetShared**
# pointer mismatch here).
#
# Access pattern note (2026-07-19): the Some-arm reads the inner Big via
# `alive.get()`, NOT `alive.data`. Direct field access through a Shared is
# rejected (E_NoFieldFound) — Shared is a NonDerefContainer whose design access
# is `.get()` (owner ruling, RV-A `afd5c327`); a wrapper `.field` shorthand was
# never intended and used to silently read garbage. The read still dereferences
# the (potentially freed) allocation, so the UAF defeat is exercised unchanged:
# on the CORRECT build `upgrade()` returns None (refcount hit 0) and we print
# -1; only a regressed upgrade that hands out a Shared into freed memory would
# reach the Some arm, and `alive.get()` would then trip the sanitizer.

struct Big:
    Vector[int] data

void drop_all(Shared[Big] !s):
    pass

void main():
    Shared[Big] s = Shared[Big](Big([1, 2, 3]))
    Weak[Big] w = s.downgrade()
    drop_all(!s)
    Option[Shared[Big]] upgraded = w.upgrade()
    match upgraded:
        case Some(alive): print(alive.get().data.get(0).unwrap())
        case None(): print(-1)
